  "worked_solution": "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, ${a}_{1}=49.$ To find the value of $r,$ we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-7}{49}=-\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=49,N=13$ and $r=-\\frac{1}{7}$ into our formula, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{13} & =49\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(-\\frac{1}{7})}^{13})}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{7}))} \\\\  & =49\\cdot \\frac{(1+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{13})}{(1+\\frac{1}{7})} \\\\  & =49\\cdot \\frac{(1+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{13})}{(\\frac{8}{7})} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{(\\frac{8}{7})}\\cdot (1+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{13}) \\\\  & =\\frac{343}{8}\\cdot (1+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{13})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the expression above with $k(1+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{13}),$ we obtain $k=\\frac{343}{8}.$",
